  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly through Sunday morning with the last of lake \neffect clouds diminishing this afternoon.\n\nChilly northwest flow will continue this morning, with lake induced \ninstability and upslope flow supporting a few widely scattered \nclouds this afternoon. Strong subsidence from a building high to the \nsoutheast of the region will continue to ridge this afternoon across \nthe region and diminish the lake clouds from west to east this \nafternoon. The cold airmass will only support highs in the mid to \nupper 40s for lower elevations and lower 40s higher terrain despite \nthe afternoon sun.\n\nHigh pressure will build from the Ohio Valley into PA and the Mid \nAtlantic tonight. Associated dry air and subsidence will support \nclear skies across western and north-central New York. The clear \nskies will certainly support radiational cooling overnight, but with \nthe high centered south of the region a light breeze may persist in \nmost areas and limit radiational cooling to some extent. A Freeze \nWarning remains in effect for areas where the growing season is \nstill active, but the prospect of a light breeze persisting lowers \nconfidence that temps will drop below freezing in some areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An extended period of dry and warmer weather begins \nSunday afternoon and likely lasts through next weekend.\n\nStrong high pressure will drift to the Mid Atlantic and New England \nby Sunday afternoon, allowing southwest flow to return and quickly \nremove the chilly airmass from the region. Highs will reach the \nlower 60s for lower elevations and mid 50s for higher terrain and \nthe North Country after a chilly start.\n\nSunday will begin an extended stretch of dry and sunny weather that \nwill likely last all the way through next weekend. High pressure \nsurface and aloft will take up residence across much of the central \nand eastern CONUS, with the pattern stagnating for an extended \nperiod of time. This will yield dry weather and plenty of sunshine \neach day for the foreseeable future. A cutoff low will be trapped \nwithin the ridge just off the Carolina and Mid Atlantic coast all \nweek, but model and ensemble consensus keeps this system well \nremoved from the eastern Great Lakes.",
